{"name":"Iceberg Green","hex":"#8c9c92","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"iceberg","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The seaward end of a glacier. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A huge mass of ocean-floating ice which has broken off a glacier or ice shelf"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/iceberg"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Iceberg","blocks":[{"text":"An iceberg is a piece of fresh water ice more than 15 meters long that has broken off a glacier or an ice shelf and is floating freely in open water. Smaller chunks of floating glacially derived ice are called \"growlers\" or \"bergy bits\". Much of an iceberg is below the water's surface, which led to the expression \"tip of the iceberg\" to illustrate a small part of a larger unseen issue. Icebergs are considered a serious maritime hazard."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Iceberg_in_the_Arctic_with_its_underside_exposed.jpg/330px-Iceberg_in_the_Arctic_with_its_underside_exposed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iceberg","wikidata":"Q47568"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Blue_Tabular_Iceberg.jpg/500px-Blue_Tabular_Iceberg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Blue_Tabular_Iceberg.jpg/1280px-Blue_Tabular_Iceberg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Blue_Tabular_Iceberg.jpg/500px-Blue_Tabular_Iceberg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tabular iceberg","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlue_Tabular_Iceberg.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Iceberg_from_overhead_IMG_0320.jpg/500px-Iceberg_from_overhead_IMG_0320.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Iceberg_from_overhead_IMG_0320.jpg/1280px-Iceberg_from_overhead_IMG_0320.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Iceberg_from_overhead_IMG_032